        "Making a debate public — when you publish it or, on the Free plan, when it finishes — and keeping it public after you close your account — Debate content, pseudonym; after you close your account, the public text, shown without your pseudonym — Contract — Art. 6(1)(b): a public debate is the service you asked for. After you close your account, legitimate interests — point (f) of the same paragraph: those of the people who added to the debate or spent credit digging into it, of readers who rely on it, and ours in a public record that stays whole; once your name is gone the text normally holds no personal data. You may object; section 10. For sensitive data about you, Art. 9(2)(e) — data you have manifestly made public",
        "Checking the synthesis of each finished debate for four categories — possible terrorism, hate speech of any kind, tendencies to suicide or harming other people, and crafting weapons or drugs — showing you the flag on your own debate, requiring a person's approval before a flagged debate can be published, and deleting a debate whose synthesis it finds touching “tendencies to suicide or harming other people” (section 6) — The debate's synthesis, sent to the model; the stored result: the verdict, the categories found and the time, plus technical facts that hold no content (which model and which version of the check produced the result, how many calls it made and, if the check could not run, why) — Legitimate interests — Art. 6(1)(f): keeping these kinds of content off our public pages. For the category “tendencies to suicide or harming other people”, the explicit consent you give on a separate screen before your first debate — Art. 9(2)(a) (section 3)",
