      { kind: "p", lead: "What deletion actually does.", text: "What deletion actually does. Your private debates and account data are encrypted under keys specific to your account and to each debate. Deleting your account destroys those keys in the live service, after which the encrypted records there cannot be read by us or anyone else without a copy of those keys from one of our backups, and we delete your account record. The key to a public debate's public copy is not destroyed at deletion: the debate stays public (section 6). We describe this as deletion because that is its effect in the live service, and we hold a documented assessment behind it; if you want to know more, ask. Four things to know: the security audit trail is append-only and is not deleted, but contains no readable identifiers of you; a small number of older debates predate our current encryption scheme, and if that applies to your account we tell you what closure achieves for them; copies of data already sent to an AI provider are governed by that provider's retention terms in the Register, not by our deletion; and some of our backups hold the keys together with the records, so what a backup copy holds can be restored until that copy is removed, and the copies made when we upload a new version of the service are kept until we remove them (the row on backups above)." }
